# Line endings, declared once, because not declaring them cost a release.
#
# Three separate bugs in one day traced to the same thing: this repo stored a
# mixture of LF and CRLF, and `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows meant a working
# copy never matched the blob. Each one looked like a different bug, and all
# three were invisible in their own error output, because a carriage return
# does not show up in a diff you read on a terminal:
#
#   1. `gen-readme.mjs` anchored its region regex on `-->\n` and reported all
#      fourteen generated regions as "never placed" on this machine.
#   2. `check-readme.mjs` digested shot sources as raw bytes, so the banner's
#      digest recorded on Windows could never equal the one computed on Linux —
#      an UNCLEARABLE staleness alarm, since recapturing re-recorded the same
#      wrong digest.
#   3. `gen-spec.mjs --check` compared generated LF against whatever was on
#      disk, so the same commit was "up to date" on the Linux gates job and
#      "stale" on the Windows build one job later.
#
# All three now normalise on read, which is the defence that works on a
# checkout somebody already has. This file is the other half: it stops the
# working copies disagreeing at all.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than `text=auto` alone: this project is developed on Windows
# but every gate runs on Linux, and generated artefacts are compared BYTE FOR
# BYTE against what a generator emits. A generator emits `\n`; anything that
# checks out `\r\n` is a file the check cannot agree with.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never touches a byte. Getting this wrong is worse than the
# problem above: "normalising" a PNG rewrites bytes inside the image.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.bmp binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
*.db binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
